  "class_weight_diagnostic": {
    "training_delay_rate": 0.202978,
    "weight_ratio": 3.9266422962094416,
    "correction": "divide the predicted odds by the weight ratio",
    "assumptions": "It assumes three things. (1) The only effect of class weighting is to multiply the fitted odds by (1-p)/p -- exactly true for a logistic model with a free intercept, approximate for boosted trees. (2) The model is otherwise correctly specified, so a single intercept shift is enough; our residuals show it is not, because the scores are also over-dispersed. (3) The period being scored has the same base rate as training, which fails here: training runs at 0.2030 and September at 0.1300. So we report it as a diagnostic that confirms the CAUSE of the miscalibration, and let the fitted methods supply the correction.",
    "fitted_on_test_bins": false
  },
  "independence_caveat": "The September assessment is not a fully independent end-to-end evaluation. The model and its settings were chosen on this same validation month in step 03, so the scores being calibrated here were already tuned against September. Splitting the month by date keeps the CALIBRATOR honest -- it never sees the days it is judged on -- but it cannot undo the earlier model selection. A fully independent figure would need a further untouched period, and we are not willing to spend the test set on it.",
  "published_test_metrics_unchanged": "The November-December results in artifacts/test_results.json stand exactly as originally recorded. No calibrator is applied to them, and no claim is made that the mean squared error, the average gap or the probabilities measured there have improved. Every mapping here preserves the order of flights, so none could change the test curve areas, precision or recall in any case.",
